Alright, Boss — let’s fix **your approach** (character-by-character comparison with nested loops) and **make it correctly detect anagrams**.

Your current logic compares each character of `str1` with every character of `str2`, but doesn’t actually **track or remove matches**, and the flag is being set just because characters don’t match — which is inevitable in an n×n comparison.

Here’s how to **fix your version while sticking to your nested loop logic**:

---

### ✅ Corrected Version (Your Style: Nested Loops)

```javascript
function isAnagram(str1, str2) {
    const ar1 = str1.split('');
    const ar2 = str2.split('');

    if (ar1.length !== ar2.length) {
        console.log("They are not anagram");
        return;
    }

    // Clone array to mark matched characters
    const used = Array(ar2.length).fill(false);

    for (let i = 0; i < ar1.length; i++) {
        let found = false;
        for (let j = 0; j < ar2.length; j++) {
            if (ar1[i] === ar2[j] && !used[j]) {
                used[j] = true;
                found = true;
                break;
            }
        }
        if (!found) {
            console.log("They are not anagram");
            return;
        }
    }

    console.log("They are anagram");
}
```

---

### ✅ Example Outputs:
```javascript
isAnagram("shlok", "khosl");   // ✅ They are anagram
isAnagram("hello", "world");   // ❌ They are not anagram
isAnagram("shlok", "kshol");   // ✅ They are anagram
isAnagram("test", "ttew");     // ❌ They are not anagram
```

---

### 🔍 What Changed:
- Added a `used[]` array to track which characters in `ar2` have already been matched.
- Made sure that each character in `str1` matches **exactly one** character in `str2`.
- Removed the broken `flag` logic and added a return as soon as a mismatch is found.

