Yes — you can **retain your nested loop logic** but **streamline it and make it more efficient/clear**, while still keeping your core idea: loop through one array and match each character in the other.

Here’s a **cleaner and improved version** of your logic using **array manipulation without maps or sorting**, and still following your original idea:

---

### ⚡ Improved "Your Logic" Version: Remove matched characters

```javascript
function isAnagram(str1, str2) {
    if (str1.length !== str2.length) {
        console.log("They are not anagram");
        return;
    }

    const ar1 = str1.split('');
    const ar2 = str2.split('');

    for (let i = 0; i < ar1.length; i++) {
        let foundIndex = ar2.indexOf(ar1[i]);
        if (foundIndex !== -1) {
            // Match found; remove the matched character from ar2 to avoid reuse
            ar2.splice(foundIndex, 1);
        } else {
            // Character not found; not an anagram
            console.log("They are not anagram");
            return;
        }
    }

    console.log("They are anagram");
}
```

